  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAs the new week progresses, the repeated passage of northwest-\nflow perturbations to the north of the forecast area will\neventually re-amplify the western-ridge/eastern-trough pattern.\nStronger vorticity maxima will dig southeastward Tuesday night\nand Wednesday night through the central U.S. Eventually, these\nshortwave troughs will enable a synoptic cold front to sag\nsouthward into the forecast area. This will lead to a stout\ncooling trend for the middle and latter portions of next week.\nAdditionally, the faster upper-level flow will sink southward\ninto the central Plains, leading to round after round of\nvorticity maxima passing over the region. As a result, storm\nchances will greatly increase Wednesday through at least Friday\nacross the CWA.\n\nThis pattern change is favorable for a swath of heavy rainfall.\nCoarser physical/AI models show such swaths of heavier QPF (2-4+\ninches) in a northwest-to-southeast swath near the storm track\nthat sets up. Of course, given the extended time range, these\nswaths have considerable spatial variability given the lower\npredictability. However, the general area of these swaths is\nsomewhere between central Iowa (ECMWF) to central Missouri \n(AIGFS/CMC) or areas in between (GFS/EC-AIFS). The key here is\nthat this will be a period to watch for flooding potential, and\nportions of our forecast area may be at heightened risk later\nnext week.\n\nOf course, another question mark will be how quickly the heat of\nthe early week period dwindles with time. Uncertainty increases\nconsiderably Wednesday and Thursday, with models tending to be a\nlittle too conservative with the southward push of cooler air.\nThis will be largely dependent on the extent and evolution of\nseveral convective systems in the early-to-mid week period. As a\nresult, substantial temperature and PoP forecast adjustments \nmay be required for the mid-week period.",
